cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Valdrada: a real-data, real-time digital twin of New York City"
message: >-
  If you use this software, the compiled city or imagery rendered with it, please
  cite it as below. Valdrada is developed and
  maintained by Mehmet Kerem Turkcan.
type: software
version: "0.6.1"
abstract: >-
  A city-scale digital twin of the five boroughs of New York City compiled from
  public municipal records. 1,085,937 buildings, joined to tax-lot records and
  facade-inspection filings, are extruded, classified into 16 facade typologies
  and dressed with procedural architectural detail. Street centrelines with their
  recorded widths, lane counts, directions and speed limits become the roadbed,
  sidewalks, markings and the lane graph on which traffic is simulated. The city
  is compiled offline into 3,532 binary 512 m tiles and 261 far-field 2,048 m
  tiles, which a WebGL2 client streams within a 1 km detail radius and a 13 km
  far-field radius. A CARLA-style server and Python API provide synchronous
  stepping, vehicle and pedestrian control, and RGB, semantic, instance and depth
  sensors with per-object visible and amodal boxes. A packaged Unreal Engine 5
  client renders the same simulation, run by a headless core, through the same API.
authors:
  - family-names: Turkcan
    given-names: Mehmet Kerem
    affiliation: Columbia University
repository-code: "https://github.com/mkturkcan/valdrada"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - digital twin
  - New York City
  - urban simulation
  - synthetic data
  - procedural generation
  - semantic segmentation
  - WebGL
  - open data
references:
